{%- comment -%}
  The hero — a single looping film, full bleed, and nothing over it.

  This REPLACES the two-slide photograph carousel on the home page, asked for
  2026-09-13: "hapus semua trs ganti dgn video … video dulu skrg, simplicity
  dulu". So there is no eyebrow, no heading, no body copy and no CTA pair here.
  `sections/hero-carousel.liquid` is still in the repo and still works; it is
  simply not placed on any template any more. See its own comment for why it is
  kept rather than deleted.

  WHAT CARRIES THE PAGE'S h1 NOW. The carousel put an h1 on every slide, and
  taking the copy out would have left the home page with no h1 at all — the
  next heading down is New arrivals, which is an h2 inside its own section. So
  this band carries a screen-reader-only h1. It is not a copy slot in disguise:
  the film already has the words "Wear Label" rendered into it about a third of
  the way in, so the h1 is the text equivalent of something a sighted reader
  actually sees, and it defaults to the shop's own name rather than to a
  sentence somebody invented. Do not style it visible to "fix" an empty-looking
  band — the band is not empty, it is a film.

  THE BAND'S HEIGHT IS DECIDED BY WHERE THE WORDMARK IS. The film is 1920x1080
  and it renders the words "Wear Label" into the top 7% of its own frame, so
  the rule is: never crop the top.

  That reads differently at each end of the range, and it is why the band is
  not one shape:

    - `md` and up the band is 16:9 exactly, so nothing is cropped at all. A
      fixed 600px band on a 1440px screen would have taken 105px off the top
      and the wordmark with it, which is the one thing in the film that says
      whose film it is. The price is a tall hero — 1440 wide is 810 tall — and
      it is paid on purpose: a film asked to be the whole first screen.
    - Below `md` the band is 60svh instead, and `object-cover` crops it
      SIDEWAYS rather than vertically. Covering a 390x506 box from a 16:9
      source scales the frame to 900px wide and trims the left and right edges,
      so the full height survives — wordmark included — and so does the model,
      who is near the centre of frame throughout. 16:9 at 390px wide would have
      been a 219px strip, which is a band, not a hero.

  svh rather than vh, for the same reason the carousel used it: hiding the
  browser's URL bar must not resize the hero mid-scroll.

  A portrait cut of the film would beat the sideways crop and there is not one.
  Ask for one rather than reaching for a different aspect here.

  THREE SOURCES, in precedence order, and each one is a different kind of
  answer to "where does the film live":

    1. `video` — a file the merchant picked in the theme editor, hosted by
       Shopify. This is the one to grow into: Shopify transcodes it, serves
       HLS, and `video_tag` writes the markup. It is empty today because the
       film was handed over as a file in the repo, not uploaded to the store.
    2. `asset` — a file in the theme's own assets/. This is what ships:
       hero-video.mp4, 1920x1080, 16s, H.264, 4.5MB, audio stripped.
    3. neither — the labelled placeholder at the band's exact size, like every
       other empty slot on this site.

  NO SOUND, and it is not an oversight. A hero film autoplays, and a browser
  will only autoplay a muted one, so the audio track would never be heard
  without a control to unmute it — and an unmute button is a decision nobody
  has made. The track was therefore dropped at encode, which is also 250KB off
  the file. The master still has it: asset/Video/main video/Wear Label.mov.

  REDUCED MOTION. A 16-second loop is exactly the kind of motion the setting
  exists to stop, and it cannot be stopped by shortening a duration. So the
  video is hidden and a still — the film's own first frame — is shown in its
  place, in CSS, in theme-src/theme.css. CSS rather than script because a
  reader who asked for less motion must not have to run JavaScript to be
  obeyed. theme.js then pauses the hidden video as well, which is the part that
  saves the download; that is the enhancement, and the swap is the fallback.

  TWO STILLS, and they are different frames on purpose — this was got wrong
  once and caught in a screenshot, so it is worth the paragraph.

  The POSTER is frame zero. It shows while the file is still arriving and then
  gives way to playback, so it has to be the frame playback starts on; any
  better-composed frame would put a visible jump at the moment the film begins.
  Frame zero is a wide shot of a white stair with the model entering at the
  right edge — a fine opening frame and a poor photograph.

  The REDUCED-MOTION STILL is not transitional. It is the whole hero, for as
  long as that reader is on the page, so it is a composed frame from about
  twelve seconds in: the model centre-frame, full length, with the film's own
  wordmark above her. Centre-frame matters twice over, because below `md` the
  band crops sideways (see above) — a frame with the model against the right
  edge would crop her out of her own hero.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign heading = section.settings.heading | default: shop.name
  assign poster_url = section.settings.poster | asset_url
  assign still_name = section.settings.still | default: section.settings.poster
  assign still_url = still_name | asset_url
  assign video_url = section.settings.asset | asset_url
-%}

<section class="relative w-full overflow-hidden bg-tone">
  <h1 class="sr-only">{{ heading }}</h1>

  <div data-hero-video class="relative h-[60svh] min-h-80 w-full md:h-auto md:aspect-video">
    {%- if section.settings.video != blank -%}
      {{
        section.settings.video
        | video_tag:
          image_size: '1920x',
          autoplay: true,
          loop: true,
          muted: true,
          controls: false,
          preload: 'metadata'
      }}
    {%- elsif section.settings.asset != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        `playsinline` is what keeps iOS from taking the film fullscreen the
        moment it autoplays; `muted` is what lets it autoplay at all. Both are
        required, not stylistic.

        `preload="metadata"` rather than `auto`: autoplay starts the download
        anyway, and `auto` additionally tells a browser that has suppressed
        autoplay — a data-saver mode, a background tab — to fetch 4.5MB nobody
        is going to watch.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <video
        autoplay
        muted
        loop
        playsinline
        preload="metadata"
        poster="{{ poster_url }}"
        width="1920"
        height="1080"
        {% if section.settings.description != blank %}
          aria-label="{{ section.settings.description | escape }}"
        {% endif %}
      >
        <source src="{{ video_url }}" type="video/mp4">
      </video>
    {%- else -%}
      {% render 'media', image: nil, fill: true, sizes: '100vw', label: 'Hero film', class: 'rounded-none' %}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    The reduced-motion still. It is in the markup for everyone and hidden for
    everyone but the readers who asked for it — there is no media query in
    Liquid, and a still that only script can produce is a still that reduced
    motion does not reliably get. `alt` is deliberately empty: the h1 above
    already carries the name, so a second announcement of the same film is
    noise rather than information.

    It falls back to the poster when no still of its own is set, which is the
    right failure: a transitional frame shown permanently is worse than the
    composed one and far better than an empty band.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if still_name != blank -%}
    <img
      data-hero-still
      src="{{ still_url }}"
      alt=""
      width="1920"
      height="1080"
      class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover"
    >
  {%- endif -%}
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Hero video",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "video",
      "id": "video",
      "label": "Film",
      "info": "A video uploaded to the store. Overrides the shipped file below and is served by Shopify."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "asset",
      "label": "Shipped film filename",
      "default": "hero-video.mp4",
      "info": "A file in the theme's assets/. Used when no film is uploaded above."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "poster",
      "label": "Poster filename",
      "default": "hero-video-poster.webp",
      "info": "A file in the theme's assets/. Shown while the film loads, and shown instead of it for readers who have asked for reduced motion."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "still",
      "label": "Reduced-motion still filename",
      "default": "hero-video-still.webp",
      "info": "A file in the theme's assets/. Shown instead of the film to readers who have asked for reduced motion. A composed frame, not the poster — it is the whole hero for them. Blank falls back to the poster."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Screen-reader heading",
      "info": "The page's h1. It is not painted — the film carries the wordmark. Blank falls back to the store name."
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "description",
      "label": "Film description",
      "default": "A short film for Wear Label: a model walks through a sunlit courtyard in a white shirt and wide-leg trousers.",
      "info": "Read out in place of the film by a screen reader."
    }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Hero video" }]
}
{% endschema %}
